//     `destroy` method in order to free it after use, either by calling it explicitly
//     or by using a higher-level helper like the `use` method. Failing to do so risks
//     leaking the underlying Rust struct.
//
//   * We can't assume that calling code will do the right thing, and must be prepared
//     to handle Kotlin method calls executing concurrently with or even after a call to
//     `destroy`, and to handle multiple (possibly concurrent!) calls to `destroy`.
//
//   * We must never allow Rust code to operate on the underlying Rust struct after
//     the destructor has been called, and must never call the destructor more than once.
//     Doing so may trigger memory unsafety.
//
//   * To mitigate many of the risks of leaking memory and use-after-free unsafety, a `Cleaner`
//     is implemented to call the destructor when the Kotlin object becomes unreachable.
//     This is done in a background thread. This is not a panacea, and client code should be aware that
//      1. the thread may starve if some there are objects that have poorly performing
//     `drop` methods or do significant work in their `drop` methods.
//      2. the thread is shared across the whole library. This can be tuned by using `android_cleaner = true`,
//         or `android = true` in the [`kotlin` section of the `uniffi.toml` file](https://mozilla.github.io/uniffi-rs/kotlin/configuration.html).
//
// If we try to implement this with mutual exclusion on access to the handle, there is the
// possibility of a race between a method call and a concurrent call to `destroy`:
//
//    * Thread A starts a method call, reads the value of the handle, but is interrupted
//      before it can pass the handle over the FFI to Rust.
//    * Thread B calls `destroy` and frees the underlying Rust struct.
//    * Thread A resumes, passing the already-read handle value to Rust and triggering
//      a use-after-free.
//
// One possible solution would be to use a `ReadWriteLock`, with each method call taking
// a read lock (and thus allowed to run concurrently) and the special `destroy` method
// taking a write lock (and thus blocking on live method calls). However, we aim not to
// generate methods with any hidden blocking semantics, and a `destroy` method that might
// block if called incorrectly seems to meet that bar.
//
// So, we achieve our goals by giving each instance an associated `AtomicLong` counter to track
// the number of in-flight method calls, and an `AtomicBoolean` flag to indicate whether `destroy`
// has been called. These are updated according to the following rules:
//
//    * The initial value of the counter is 1, indicating a live object with no in-flight calls.
//      The initial value for the flag is false.
//
//    * At the start of each method call, we atomically check the counter.
//      If it is 0 then the underlying Rust struct has already been destroyed and the call is aborted.
//      If it is nonzero them we atomically increment it by 1 and proceed with the method call.
//
//    * At the end of each method call, we atomically decrement and check the counter.
//      If it has reached zero then we destroy the underlying Rust struct.
//
//    * When `destroy` is called, we atomically flip the flag from false to true.
//      If the flag was already true we silently fail.
//      Otherwise we atomically decrement and check the counter.
//      If it has reached zero then we destroy the underlying Rust struct.
//
// Astute readers may observe that this all sounds very similar to the way that Rust's `Arc<T>` works,
// and indeed it is, with the addition of a flag to guard against multiple calls to `destroy`.
//
// The overall effect is that the underlying Rust struct is destroyed only when `destroy` has been
// called *and* all in-flight method calls have completed, avoiding violating any of the expectations
// of the underlying Rust code.
//
// This makes a cleaner a better alternative to _not_ calling `destroy()` as
// and when the object is finished with, but the abstraction is not perfect: if the Rust object's `drop`
// method is slow, and/or there are many objects to cleanup, and it's on a low end Android device, then the cleaner
// thread may be starved, and the app will leak memory.
//
// In this case, `destroy`ing manually may be a better solution.
//
// The cleaner can live side by side with the manual calling of `destroy`. In the order of responsiveness, uniffi objects
// with Rust peers are reclaimed:
//
// 1. By calling the `destroy` method of the object, which calls `rustObject.free()`. If that doesn't happen:
// 2. When the object becomes unreachable, AND the Cleaner thread gets to call `rustObject.free()`. If the thread is starved then:
// 3. The memory is reclaimed when the process terminates.
//
// [1] https://stackoverflow.com/questions/24376768/can-java-finalize-an-object-when-it-is-still-in-scope/24380219
//


/**
 * Foreign aliases share this single serialized owner. No callback API is exported;
 * owned synchronous results precede the next submission and survive disposal.
 */
public interface NativeSessionInterface {
    
    fun `cancelCommands`(`token`: kotlin.String)
    
    /**
     * Ordered idempotent release of an uncommitted plan. After disposal: Disposed.
     */
    fun `cancelEdits`(`token`: kotlin.String)
    
    /**
     * Capture all endpoints against one observed revision. Validate every target
     * before retaining anything, so an invalid endpoint cannot leak anchor slots.
     */
    fun `captureAnchors`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `inputs`: List<AnchorInput>): List<NativeSessionAnchor>
    
    /**
     * Exact current target applicability via the authoritative planner; retains no plan.
     */
    fun `checkCommands`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `commandsJson`: kotlin.String, `origin`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong)
    
    /**
     * Detached current metadata. Target-specific enablement requires check_commands.
     */
    fun `commandCatalog`(`now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeCommandCatalog
    
    fun `commitCommands`(`token`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionCommit?
    
    /**
     * Single use, including failed admission. Current permission/revision is checked
     * again by EditService. Cancellation cannot interrupt an accepted commit.
     */
    fun `commitEdits`(`token`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionCommit?
    
    /**
     * Idempotent across aliases. Waits for accepted work and drops the real Session,
     * all plans and native store on the owning executor before returning.
     */
    fun `dispose`()
    
    /**
     * Exact stack availability for native menus/keys. The observation is advisory:
     * undo/redo still recheck current permission, admission and the supplied revision.
     * Disposal returns Disposed, as with every other read on this handle.
     */
    fun `historyState`(`now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionHistory
    
    /**
     * Owned committed outbox records; frozen Sessions cannot publish pending updates.
     */
    fun `pendingUpdates`(): List<NativeSessionPeerUpdate>
    
    /**
     * Same portable EditorCommand array and anchor tokens as the shared engine.
     * All parsing, anchor lookup and EditService validation precede retaining a plan.
     */
    fun `prepareCommands`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `commandsJson`: kotlin.String, `origin`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativePreparedCommands
    
    /**
     * Whole-edit batches use the existing atomic EditService planner. Revision bytes
     * are equality validators supplied by read(), never authorization credentials.
     */
    fun `prepareEdits`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `edits`: List<NativeSessionEdit>, `origin`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): kotlin.String
    
    fun `read`(`now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionRead
    
    /**
     * Trusted-host ingress over the actual EditService/Session, after host security admission.
     * This accepts neither unauthenticated network frames nor encrypted packets.
     */
    fun `receivePeer`(`update`: NativeSessionPeerUpdate): NativeSessionCommit
    
    fun `recover`()
    
    fun `recoveryExport`(): NativeSessionRecovery
    
    fun `redo`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionCommit?
    
    /**
     * Idempotent for unknown/foreign tokens; cleanup is available during recovery.
     */
    fun `releaseAnchor`(`token`: kotlin.String)
    
    /**
     * Trusted host reserves an actual peer writer once, never an authentication grant.
     */
    fun `reservePeerWriter`(`writer`: kotlin.UInt)
    
    /**
     * Resolve the retained shared sticky position against the current committed
     * revision, including deleted and unresolvable outcomes without local fallback.
     */
    fun `resolveAnchor`(`token`: kotlin.String): NativeSessionAnchorRead
    
    /**
     * Establish/reset this handle's incremental cursor. Recovery-required state
     * cannot mint an acknowledged projection; salvage remains recoveryExport.
     */
    fun `richSnapshot`(): NativeSessionProjection
    
    /**
     * Trusted host opt-in for bounded inert image/reference edits. This grants
     * neither write permission nor network/resolver authority, and is not stored.
     */
    fun `setAtomicContentPolicy`(`images`: kotlin.Boolean, `references`: kotlin.Boolean)
    
    /**
     * Trusted host permission replacement, serialized with every prepared admission.
     */
    fun `setPermission`(`writable`: kotlin.Boolean, `expiresAt`: kotlin.ULong?)
    
    /**
     * Trusted host table codec policy, independent of write permission. Defaults
     * to disabled on every create/open. Retained plans recheck it at commit.
     */
    fun `setTablePolicy`(`enabled`: kotlin.Boolean)
    
    /**
     * A single bounded consumer shared by all aliases. Failed reads never drain.
     * Overflow requires richSnapshot; no event is silently omitted or coalesced.
     */
    fun `takeRichPatches`(`afterSequence`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionPatches
    
    fun `undo`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionCommit?
    
    /**
     * Exact authenticated application receipt; only the existing atomic store path prunes.
     */
    fun `acceptHostReceipt`(`token`: kotlin.String, `receipt`: NativeHostReceipt, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeHostReceiptResult
    
    /**
     * Trusted host grants an exclusive lease, with no local write permission change.
     */
    fun `attachHostTransport`(`options`: NativeHostTransportOptions, `now`: kotlin.ULong): kotlin.String
    
    /**
     * Detach/revoke only this connection. A stale event cannot revoke a newer lease.
     */
    fun `detachHostTransport`(`token`: kotlin.String)
    
    /**
     * Existing Session y-protocols flush. Partial queueing on backpressure is retained.
     */
    fun `flushHostTransport`(`token`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): kotlin.ULong
    
    /**
     * Page over exact durable pending identities without manufacturing receipts.
     */
    fun `flushHostTransportPage`(`token`: kotlin.String, `after`: NativeHostReceipt?, `maxFrames`: kotlin.UInt, `now`: kotlin.ULong): kotlin.ULong
    
    /**
     * Existing trusted-host journal admission, additionally bound to the active lease.
     */
    fun `receiveHostPeer`(`token`: kotlin.String, `update`: NativeSessionPeerUpdate, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeSessionCommit
    
    /**
     * Pull copied outbound frames; delivery is never a remote durable acknowledgment.
     */
    fun `takeHostFrames`(`token`: kotlin.String, `maxFrames`: kotlin.UInt, `now`: kotlin.ULong): List<NativeHostFrame>
    
    /**
     * Queue a coalesced pinned awareness frame; queue backpressure retains state.
     */
    fun `flushHostPresence`(`token`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): kotlin.Boolean
    
    /**
     * Resolve relative cursors against current committed content, expiring peers.
     */
    fun `hostPresence`(`token`: kotlin.String, `now`: kotlin.ULong): List<NativePeerPresence>
    
    /**
     * Host authenticates participant routing before supplying the existing wire.
     */
    fun `receiveHostPresence`(`token`: kotlin.String, `frame`: NativeHostFrame, `now`: kotlin.ULong)
    
    /**
     * None means online without selection; present=false emits an explicit leave.
     * Both tokens must belong to this worker. No local permission is granted.
     */
    fun `setHostPresence`(`token`: kotlin.String, `anchor`: kotlin.String?, `focus`: kotlin.String?, `present`: kotlin.Boolean, `now`: kotlin.ULong)
    
    /**
     * Awareness uses a separate bounded owning transport queue from durable sync.
     */
    fun `takeHostPresenceFrames`(`token`: kotlin.String, `maxFrames`: kotlin.UInt, `now`: kotlin.ULong): List<NativeHostFrame>
    
    /**
     * Explicit acknowledged-state capture; never called from typing/edit/history hooks.
     */
    fun `captureReviewRevision`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `name`: kotlin.String, `requiredKeyProfile`: kotlin.String?): NativeReviewRevision
    
    fun `compareReviewRevisions`(`before`: NativeReviewRevision, `after`: NativeReviewRevision, `beforeKeyProfile`: kotlin.String?, `afterKeyProfile`: kotlin.String?, `maxBytes`: kotlin.ULong): NativeReviewComparison
    
    fun `readReviewRevision`(`revision`: NativeReviewRevision, `availableKeyProfile`: kotlin.String?): NativeReviewRead
    
    /**
     * Tightening immediately prunes; widening cannot recreate evicted history.
     */
    fun `setReviewRetention`(`maxRevisions`: kotlin.UInt, `maxTotalBytes`: kotlin.ULong)
    
    /**
     * Canonical shared decoder/validator. No native semantic parser or author inference.
     */
    fun `validateChangeModel`(`bytes`: kotlin.ByteArray, `maxBytes`: kotlin.ULong): NativeChangeModel
    
    fun `cancelTransfer`(`token`: kotlin.String)
    
    /**
     * Ordinary paste never deletes a source. For cut, acknowledge the clipboard;
     * for cross-document deletion, supply an opaque qualified destination receipt.
     * Every attempt consumes the plan, including rejected or stale admission.
     */
    fun `commitTransfer`(`token`: kotlin.String, `clipboardAcknowledged`: kotlin.Boolean, `destinationReceipt`: NativeTransferReceipt?, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeTransferCommit
    
    fun `exportTransfer`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `range`: NativeTransferRange, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativeTransferExport
    
    fun `prepareCut`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `range`: NativeTransferRange, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativePreparedTransfer
    
    fun `preparePaste`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `range`: NativeTransferRange, `input`: NativeTransferInput, `source`: NativeTransferSource?, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativePreparedTransfer
    
    fun `prepareTransferMove`(`revision`: kotlin.ByteArray, `source`: NativeTransferRange, `destination`: NativeTransferRange, `now`: kotlin.ULong): NativePreparedTransfer
    
    /**
     * Trusted host registration of the existing callout codec. No resolver or
     * network authority; retained transfers recheck this setting at admission.
     */
    fun `setCalloutPolicy`(`enabled`: kotlin.Boolean)
    
    companion object
}

/**
 * Foreign aliases share this single serialized owner. No callback API is exported;
 * owned synchronous results precede the next submission and survive disposal.
 */
open class NativeSession: Disposable, AutoCloseable, NativeSessionInterface
{

    @Suppress("UNUSED_PARAMETER")
    /**
     * @suppress
     */
    constructor(withHandle: UniffiWithHandle, handle: Long) {
        this.handle = handle
        this.cleanable = UniffiLib.CLEANER.register(this, UniffiCleanAction(handle))
    }

    /**
     * @suppress
     *
     * This constructor can be used to instantiate a fake object. Only used for tests. Any
     * attempt to actually use an object constructed this way will fail as there is no
     * connected Rust object.
     */
    @Suppress("UNUSED_PARAMETER")
    constructor(noHandle: NoHandle) {
        this.handle = 0
        this.cleanable = null
    }

    protected val handle: Long
    protected val cleanable: UniffiCleaner.Cleanable?

    private val wasDestroyed = AtomicBoolean(false)
    private val callCounter = AtomicLong(1)

    /**
     * Whether the current object has been destroyed and its reference is gone in the Rust side.
     */
    val uniffiIsDestroyed: Boolean get() = wasDestroyed.get()

    override fun destroy() {
        // Only allow a single call to this method.
        // TODO: maybe we should log a warning if called more than once?
        if (this.wasDestroyed.compareAndSet(false, true)) {
            // This decrement always matches the initial count of 1 given at creation time.
            if (this.callCounter.decrementAndGet() == 0L) {
                cleanable?.clean()
            }
        }
    }

    @Synchronized
    override fun close() {
        this.destroy()
    }

    internal inline fun <R> callWithHandle(block: (handle: Long) -> R): R {
        // Check and increment the call counter, to keep the object alive.
        // This needs a compare-and-set retry loop in case of concurrent updates.
        do {
            val c = this.callCounter.get()
            if (c == 0L) {
                throw IllegalStateException("${this.javaClass.simpleName} object has already been destroyed")
            }
            if (c == Long.MAX_VALUE) {
                throw IllegalStateException("${this.javaClass.simpleName} call counter would overflow")
            }
        } while (! this.callCounter.compareAndSet(c, c + 1L))
        // Now we can safely do the method call without the handle being freed concurrently.
        try {
            return block(this.uniffiCloneHandle())
        } finally {
            // This decrement always matches the increment we performed above.
            if (this.callCounter.decrementAndGet() == 0L) {
                cleanable?.clean()
            }
        }
    }

    // Use a static inner class instead of a closure so as not to accidentally
    // capture `this` as part of the cleanable's action.
    private class UniffiCleanAction(private val handle: Long) : Runnable {
        override fun run() {
            if (handle == 0.toLong()) {
                // Fake object created with `NoHandle`, don't try to free.
                return;
            }
            uniffiRustCall { status ->
                UniffiLib.uniffi_editac_ffi_fn_free_nativesession(handle, status)
            }
        }
    }

    /**
     * @suppress
     */
    fun uniffiCloneHandle(): Long {
        if (handle == 0.toLong()) {
            throw InternalException("uniffiCloneHandle() called on NoHandle object");
        }
        return uniffiRustCall() { status ->
            UniffiLib.uniffi_editac_ffi_fn_clone_nativesession(handle, status)
        }
    }

    
    @Throws(NativeSessionException::class)override fun `cancelCommands`(`token`: kotlin.String)
        = 
    callWithHandle {
    uniffiRustCallWithError(NativeSessionException) { _status ->
    UniffiLib.uniffi_editac_ffi_fn_method_nativesession_cancel_commands(
        it,
        
        FfiConverterString.lower(`token`),_status)
}
    }
    
    

    
    /**
     * Ordered idempotent release of an uncommitted plan. After disposal: Disposed.
     */
    @Throws(NativeSessionException::class)override fun `cancelEdits`(`token`: kotlin.String)
        = 
    callWithHandle {
